WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.600
Cloud incident response is the organized process for detecting, analyzing, containing,

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:10.660
recovering from, and learning from security incidents in cloud environments.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:15.360
It uses the same discipline as traditional incident response, but the evidence,

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:19.260
control points, and recovery actions often look different.

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:24.040
A responder may need to understand identities, policies, API activity,

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:29.380
storage permissions, network controls, serverless functions, managed services,

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:33.760
and SaaS integrations rather than only host images and network packets.

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:38.380
Traditional incident response often starts with a compromised endpoint or server.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:42.680
Cloud incidents may start with a suspicious login, a new access key,

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:47.620
an unexpected policy change, an exposed storage bucket, abnormal data transfer,

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:49.920
or automated resource creation.

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:54.380
The control plane matters because many important actions happen through provider APIs.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:59.080
A single identity with excessive permissions can change infrastructure, read data,

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:02.820
create resources, and alter logging if safeguards are weak.

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:05.440
Cloud environments are also highly distributed.

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:11.260
A single business application may use several accounts, subscriptions, projects, regions,

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:16.760
storage services, queues, databases, identity providers, and third-party integrations.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:21.300
Incident responders need to scope the incident across those boundaries without making

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:22.480
assumptions.

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:27.260
The first alert may be only one symptom of a broader identity or configuration issue.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:31.880
Good response work is authorized, documented, and evidence-preserving.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:35.940
Responders should know who approved the work, what systems are in scope,

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:39.280
what actions were taken, and what evidence was collected.

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:41.900
The goal is not only to stop immediate harm.

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:47.560
The goal is to understand what happened, protect affected data and systems, restore trust,

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:53.140
and support legal, privacy, compliance, and business decisions with defensible facts.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:57.600
Cloud incident response is therefore both technical and operational.

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:03.980
It requires preparation, visibility, clear roles, careful containment, communication,

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:05.480
and follow-through.

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:10.580
A fast reaction that destroys evidence or causes avoidable outages can create new

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:11.760
problems.

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:14.780
A strong response balances urgency with control.
